WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.462
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.462 --> 00:00:05.921
learn how stream pipelines use mapping,

00:00:05.921 --> 00:00:08.948
filtering, and a terminal operation to transform

00:00:08.948 --> 00:00:11.597
collection entries into an ordered report.

00:00:11.597 --> 00:00:14.434
A pipeline describes a sequence of operations

00:00:14.434 --> 00:00:16.074
over the source's entries.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:19.287
Reports often need cleaned data and a clear

00:00:19.287 --> 00:00:20.179
selection rule.

00:00:20.179 --> 00:00:22.914
Keeping those steps separate makes their order

00:00:22.914 --> 00:00:25.768
easier to inspect and change, while the original

00:00:25.768 --> 00:00:28.562
collection remains available to other features.

00:00:28.562 --> 00:00:31.119
A pipeline builds on the small rules you've

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:33.676
already supplied through lambdas and method

00:00:33.676 --> 00:00:34.330
references.

00:00:34.973 --> 00:00:37.942
A registration desk needs a report of names with

00:00:37.942 --> 00:00:40.541
at least four characters after surrounding

00:00:40.541 --> 00:00:41.716
spaces are removed.

00:00:41.716 --> 00:00:44.376
Its entries can contain padded names, short

00:00:44.376 --> 00:00:45.675
names, or empty text.

00:00:45.675 --> 00:00:48.582
We'll keep qualifying entries in their original

00:00:48.582 --> 00:00:51.428
relative order, with repeated matching entries

00:00:51.428 --> 00:00:51.923
allowed.

00:00:52.573 --> 00:00:55.434
The report should display each retained name and

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:56.924
then the number selected.

00:00:56.924 --> 00:00:58.891
First we'll trace a worked input.

00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:01.632
Then you'll predict the report for a different

00:01:01.632 --> 00:01:03.003
input before we run it.

00:01:03.003 --> 00:01:05.804
The original list and the completed report have

00:01:05.804 --> 00:01:06.698
different jobs.

00:01:07.373 --> 00:01:10.225
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:10.225 --> 00:01:13.343
Main.java and set up the two list types used by

00:01:13.343 --> 00:01:14.073
our report.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.851
We'll import ArrayList for the source and List

00:01:20.851 --> 00:01:21.794
for the result.

00:01:21.794 --> 00:01:24.685
Main is the class holding our program, and its

00:01:24.685 --> 00:01:27.199
main method is where the program starts.

00:01:27.199 --> 00:01:30.152
The collection setup and report statements will

00:01:30.152 --> 00:01:31.535
go inside that method.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.321
List is an interface for an ordered sequence

00:01:44.321 --> 00:01:46.524
that can contain repeated entries.

00:01:46.524 --> 00:01:49.246
We'll use it to read the completed report.

00:01:49.246 --> 00:01:52.162
First, let's create the source ArrayList with

00:01:52.162 --> 00:01:55.207
padded Nora, Kim, padded Bo, and empty text, in

00:01:55.207 --> 00:01:55.920
that order.

00:02:10.809 --> 00:02:13.859
The four add calls establish the source order.

00:02:13.859 --> 00:02:16.843
Nora has two spaces on each side; Bo has one.

00:02:16.843 --> 00:02:19.627
Empty text is a String with no characters.

00:02:19.627 --> 00:02:22.479
We need to remove surrounding spaces before

00:02:22.479 --> 00:02:25.263
deciding whether a name meets the report's

00:02:25.263 --> 00:02:26.059
length rule.

00:02:26.074 --> 00:02:28.894
Let's request a stream over names and add a

00:02:28.894 --> 00:02:31.583
mapping stage that supplies cleaned text.

00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:34.731
The selected variable will hold the final result

00:02:34.731 --> 00:02:36.699
once we complete the pipeline.

00:02:44.047 --> 00:02:46.249
A stream describes processing over the

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:48.742
collection; it isn't another list of copied

00:02:48.742 --> 00:02:49.205
entries.

00:02:49.205 --> 00:02:51.813
Map accepts the String trim method reference.

00:02:51.813 --> 00:02:54.537
Each processed String becomes the receiver of a

00:02:54.537 --> 00:02:56.972
trim call, and the returned cleaned String

00:02:56.972 --> 00:02:58.305
reaches the next stage.

00:02:58.305 --> 00:03:01.029
This doesn't replace the original entry or edit

00:03:01.029 --> 00:03:02.072
a String in place.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.163
Cleaning alone doesn't decide which names belong

00:03:05.163 --> 00:03:06.059
in the report.

00:03:06.059 --> 00:03:09.131
We'll add a filter that keeps an entry only when

00:03:09.131 --> 00:03:12.140
the cleaned value has at least four characters.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:20.002
The lambda receives the value from the preceding

00:03:20.002 --> 00:03:20.256
map.

00:03:20.256 --> 00:03:23.180
Its Boolean result controls whether that value

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:23.816
continues.

00:03:23.816 --> 00:03:26.550
Nora has four characters after cleaning and

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:26.995
passes.

00:03:26.995 --> 00:03:29.856
Kim has three, Bo has two, and empty text has

00:03:29.856 --> 00:03:31.636
zero, so those entries fail.

00:03:31.636 --> 00:03:34.688
Filtering selects entries; it doesn't store true

00:03:34.688 --> 00:03:36.150
or false in the report.

00:03:36.177 --> 00:03:39.128
The intermediate stages now describe the work.

00:03:39.128 --> 00:03:42.142
Let's complete the expression with the terminal

00:03:42.142 --> 00:03:44.772
operation that processes the pipeline and

00:03:44.772 --> 00:03:45.927
produces our list.

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:51.910
To list collects the retained values.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.528
The semicolon ends the whole declaration and

00:03:54.528 --> 00:03:57.204
assignment; the earlier line breaks only make

00:03:57.204 --> 00:03:58.394
its stages readable.

00:03:58.394 --> 00:04:01.071
This result preserves the surviving ArrayList

00:04:01.071 --> 00:04:03.094
order, including repeated matches.

00:04:03.094 --> 00:04:05.890
It is unmodifiable, so our report will read its

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.507
entries and size rather than add, remove, or

00:04:08.507 --> 00:04:09.459
replace entries.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.848
The completed result still needs to be

00:04:11.848 --> 00:04:12.471
displayed.

00:04:12.471 --> 00:04:15.212
We'll use an enhanced for loop to print each

00:04:15.212 --> 00:04:17.954
selected name, then print the result's size.

00:04:17.954 --> 00:04:20.446
These statements read selected after the

00:04:20.446 --> 00:04:21.630
pipeline completes.

00:04:35.874 --> 00:04:38.606
The loop prints one line for each retained

00:04:38.606 --> 00:04:38.996
entry.

00:04:38.996 --> 00:04:41.859
The final line labels the count as Selected.

00:04:41.859 --> 00:04:44.786
It measures the result list, not the original

00:04:44.786 --> 00:04:45.957
four-entry source.

00:04:45.957 --> 00:04:48.949
Let's click Run and inspect the worked report.

00:04:54.608 --> 00:04:57.335
The report shows Nora and a selected count of

00:04:57.335 --> 00:04:57.578
one.

00:04:57.578 --> 00:04:59.942
Mapping removed the surrounding spaces,

00:04:59.942 --> 00:05:02.790
filtering kept the cleaned four-character name,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.457
and the terminal operation produced the list

00:05:05.457 --> 00:05:06.608
that the loop read.

00:05:06.608 --> 00:05:09.518
The original list still contains all four source

00:05:09.518 --> 00:05:12.063
entries; the report didn't remove rejected

00:05:12.063 --> 00:05:13.033
entries from it.

00:05:13.095 --> 00:05:16.122
Let's keep the pipeline and printing statements

00:05:16.122 --> 00:05:16.765
unchanged.

00:05:16.765 --> 00:05:19.728
We'll replace just the first three inputs with

00:05:19.728 --> 00:05:21.853
padded Maya, Li, and padded Luis.

00:05:21.853 --> 00:05:24.300
The last input will remain empty text.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.133
This changes the data while keeping the same

00:05:27.133 --> 00:05:27.970
report rules.

00:05:37.501 --> 00:05:39.989
Before running, predict every printed line,

00:05:39.989 --> 00:05:41.147
including the count.

00:05:41.147 --> 00:05:43.635
For each original entry, identify the value

00:05:43.635 --> 00:05:46.123
after trimming and the result of the length

00:05:46.123 --> 00:05:46.471
check.

00:05:46.471 --> 00:05:49.133
Which entries reach the result, and what order

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:50.116
should they have?

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:52.026
Pause here to trace the pipeline.

00:06:00.099 --> 00:06:03.190
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:04.349
the actual report.

00:06:09.939 --> 00:06:12.923
The output shows Maya, then Luis, then Selected

00:06:12.923 --> 00:06:13.177
two.

00:06:13.177 --> 00:06:16.098
Both retained names have four characters after

00:06:16.098 --> 00:06:16.669
trimming.

00:06:16.669 --> 00:06:19.336
Li and empty text fail the same condition.

00:06:19.336 --> 00:06:22.130
Maya stays before Luis because that is their

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:23.971
relative order in the source.

00:06:23.971 --> 00:06:26.892
The count belongs to the completed result, not

00:06:26.892 --> 00:06:28.289
to the original input.

00:06:28.343 --> 00:06:30.514
You built a stream pipeline that maps input

00:06:30.514 --> 00:06:32.938
values, filters the mapped results, and collects

00:06:32.938 --> 00:06:35.159
an ordered report with a terminal operation.

00:06:35.159 --> 00:06:37.482
The position of each stage determines what its

00:06:37.482 --> 00:06:38.189
rule receives.

00:06:38.189 --> 00:06:40.259
For a transfer question, imagine checking

00:06:40.259 --> 00:06:42.379
product codes that may contain surrounding

00:06:42.379 --> 00:06:42.733
spaces.

00:06:42.733 --> 00:06:45.055
Give one input for which testing length before

00:06:45.055 --> 00:06:47.327
cleaning would change the report, and explain

00:06:47.327 --> 00:06:48.943
the order your rules should use.
